 1. each yields; with a block it returns self:         c.each { }.equal?(c) = true
 2. the class defines each, Enumerable does the rest:  instance_methods(false) [:each]; map [30, 20, 10], select [3, 1], sort [1, 2, 3], include?(2) true, min 1, sum 6, first(2) [3, 2]
 3. more for free:                                     each_slice(2) [[3, 2], [1]], each_cons(2) [[3, 2], [2, 1]], each_with_index [[3, 0], [2, 1], [1, 2]], reduce(:+) 6, to_a [3, 2, 1]
 4. each without a block is an Enumerator:             Enumerator, size 3, next 3, with_index(1) [[3, 1], [2, 2], [1, 3]]
 5. enum_for(__method__) in a plain method:            pair_sums([1, 2, 3]) is Enumerator, to_a [3, 5]
 6. Enumerator.new { |y| y << 1; y.yield 2 }:          to_a [1, 2, 3], next x3 [1, 2, 3], then StopIteration, size nil
 7. an infinite enumerator, consumed a piece at a time: take(8) [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13], first(5) [0, 1, 1, 2, 3], each_slice(3).first(2) [[0, 1, 1], [2, 3, 5]]
 8. lazy chains filters without materialising:         fib.lazy.select(&:even?).first(4) [0, 2, 8, 34], class Enumerator::Lazy, (1..).lazy.map { _1 * _1 }.first(3) [1, 4, 9]
 9. Enumerator.produce, a generator from a seed:       produce(1) { _1 * 2 }.take(5) [1, 2, 4, 8, 16]
10. Enumerable is a mixin, so the class says so:       Countdown.include?(Enumerable) true, c.is_a?(Enumerable) true
